*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
* ok = !MATWRITECSV(data, count, f, delim)
*
* DATA is the elements of the matrix, field mark delimited, as MATBUILD makes them, and COUNT the matrix's size as INMAT(matrix)
* gives it (MATBUILD leaves trailing null elements off the string).  Each element goes through FORMCSV (so a
* value with the delimiter, a double quote or a line break is quoted), the elements are joined with DELIM (what CSV.MODE gave,
* a comma as a rule) and the line is written with WRITESEQ to F.  Gives 1 if it was written and 0 if the write failed.  $INTERNAL
* only because FORMCSV is; the file was opened by OPENSEQ in the caller, where the OS-access gate was asked.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function matwritecsv(data, count, f, delim, quote, esc)
$catalog !matwritecsv

   * 20 Oct 26 dm - DELIM, QUOTE and ESC are the statement's own delimiter, quote and escape character, a null string for any it did not
   * name, which is the program's CSV.MODE setting.  They are made this call's CSV.MODE, which FORMCSV reads and which is undone when the
   * function returns (the setting goes with the program).
   if delim = '' then delim = system(1046)
   if quote = '' then quote = system(1090)
   if esc = '' then esc = system(1091)
   delim = delim[1, 1]
   csv.mode system(1043), delim, quote, esc

   * MATBUILD leaves the trailing null elements off, so the number of elements comes from the matrix's size (COUNT, what
   * INMAT(matrix) gives: the rows, or the rows and the columns joined by a value mark), not from the string
   rows = field(count, @vm, 1) + 0
   cols = field(count, @vm, 2) + 0
   if cols < 1 then cols = 1
   n = rows * cols
   if n < 1 then n = 1                   ;* a matrix of one element that is null
   line = ''
   for i = 1 to n
      if i > 1 then line := delim
      line := formcsv(data<i>)
   next i
   writeseq line to f then return 1 else return 0
end

* END-CODE
